Why the split
-------------

Creative Commons licences are the right tool for prose and the wrong
one for code, and Creative Commons says so themselves: their licences
carry no terms about source-code distribution, do not address patent
rights, and are not compatible with the established software licences.
GPL-3.0 covers exactly those three things — it is built around the
obligation to convey Corresponding Source (§6), and its §11 grants an
explicit patent licence from every contributor, which matters for a
tool that implements published measurement methods.

The reverse also holds, and it is the reason the documentation is NOT
under the GPL. A research paper, a course handout or a national
forest-inventory manual can quote and adapt PointCloudLabeler's
documentation under CC BY without inheriting a software licence and
without the obligation to distribute source alongside it. Copyleft on
prose would put a reviewer or a teacher in a position CC BY does not.

CC BY 4.0 is one-way compatible with GPL-3.0 by Creative Commons'
own compatibility determination, so documentation text may be moved
into the program; the reverse is not true, and is not intended to be.

Code samples inside the documentation
-------------------------------------

Snippets of PointCloudLabeler's own source quoted inside a
documentation file stay under the GPL, the licence of the file they
came from. Applying a
documentation licence to code by quoting it would be an accident, not
a relicensing.

What the GPL does not reach
---------------------------

Output is not a derivative work of the program that produced it. A
stand table, a CSV of per-tree measurements, an HTML report or the
figures in a paper belong to whoever made the measurements, under
whatever terms they choose. The GPL governs PointCloudLabeler and
works derived from its own code — not the forests you measure with it.

Third-party material
--------------------

Neither licence applies to the third-party assets PointCloudLabeler
bundles or embeds — the typefaces, the EPSG registry, the statically
linked libraries. Those keep their own licences, reproduced in full in
`THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.md`, as those licences require.
